<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chellah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sala Colonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chellah is a walled necropolis and ancient site on the edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, above the south bank of the Bou Regreg estuary. It began as the Phoenician and Roman town of Sala, and in the 14th century the Marinid sultans built a royal burial ground there with a mosque, tombs and a monumental gateway. Its ruins, home to nesting storks, form part of Rabat's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chellah.jpg" alt="Chellah" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Timothy A. Gonsalves. Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Ruins_Zawiya_Chellah_Rabat_Nov25_A7CR_08989.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> settled on the site has been debated by archaeologists. Jean Boube, who led some of the modern excavations, found neo-Punic artefacts from as early as the 3rd century BCE, suggesting a small trading post, and the settlement's name, Shalat, appears to come from the Punic word for rock. In the 1st century BCE an urban centre grew on hill terraces above the Bou Regreg valley under the Kingdom of Mauretania, whose client kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> and Ptolemy may have used it as a dynastic sanctuary; portrait statues of both have been found there.</p><p>After Ptolemy's death in 40 CE the region was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as the province of Mauretania Tingitana. Sala Colonia became a port city with a forum, a triumphal arch and a main road, the decumanus maximus, and inscriptions show it held the status of a municipium around the mid-2nd century. It was one of the province's two main naval outposts on the Atlantic, with Lixus. Sala began to be abandoned in the 5th century and was largely in ruins when the Muslim Arabs arrived in the 7th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Marinid enclosure is roughly pentagonal and smaller than the Roman city. Its rammed-earth walls have three gates and square towers; the main gate in the north-west, of brick and cut stone with a Kufic inscription recording its construction, shows the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> gates such as Bab er-Rouah. Inside lies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalwa">khalwa</a>, a religious complex with a mosque, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and a garden cemetery of domed tombs, among them those of Sultan Abu Sa'id, who died in 1331, and Shams al-Duha, who died in 1349. Sultans Abu Ya'qub Yusuf and Abu Thabit 'Amir were buried there in 1307 and 1308. A hammam, a pool fed by the spring of 'Ayn al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janna">Janna</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shrines complete the site.</p><p>After the Marinid period the necropolis declined. It was first pillaged by Ahmad al-Lihyani, a pretender to the Marinid throne, and many of its buildings were ruined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a>. In 1790 Sultan Moulay Yazid ordered the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> to remove the Sabbah tribe, who had taken over the enclosure, and the site was looted again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavations in the 20th century uncovered about 1.2 hectares of the Roman town around its forum. Since 2012 the site has formed part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and finds from it, including Marinid tombstones, are kept in the Museum of History and Civilizations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chellah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isa rose against his brother Muhammad and was driven into refuge at <b>Chellah</b> by Umar, who then turned on al-Qasim for refusing to join them and was rewarded with the governorship of Tangier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their madrasas in Fez, Salé and Meknes are counted among the greatest works of western Islamic architecture of the period, beside the Grand Mosque of Fes el-Jdid (1276), the Great Mosque of Taza (1294) and the <b>Chellah</b> necropolis gate of 1339.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Ifranid emirs ruled Salé and <b>Chellah</b> and fought the Barghawata.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman control covered the north of Morocco, with towns such as Volubilis, <b>Sala</b>, Lixus and Banasa linked by roads and protected by forts.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-tingitana">Mauretania Tingitana</a></li></ul></section>
